window.docContents[126].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Section-2___7___-Examples-of-algebras-___-Weyl-algebra-and-q-Weyl-algebra/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___7_intro___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.7. Examples of algebras\u0009Section 2.7: Examples of algebras — Weyl algebra and q-Weyl algebra\u0009Section 2.7: Examples of algebras — Weyl algebra and q-Weyl algebra","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Section-2___7___-Examples-of-algebras-___-Weyl-algebra-and-q-Weyl-algebra/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___7_intro___formalization"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Representations-of-quivers-as-representations-of-path-algebras/#Chapter2___Discussion_path_algebra_intro":{"contents":"It turns out that the theory of representations of quivers is a part of the theory of representations of algebras in the sense that for each quiver Q, there exists a certain algebra P_Q, called the path algebra of Q, such that a representation of the quiver Q is \"the same\" as a representation of the algebra P_Q. We shall first define the path algebra of a quiver and then justify our claim that representations of these two objects are \"the same\".\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Representations of quivers as representations of path algebras","header":"Representations of quivers as representations of path algebras","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Representations-of-quivers-as-representations-of-path-algebras/#Chapter2___Discussion_path_algebra_intro"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___6___-Characters-of-representations/Linear-independence-of-characters_SEMI_-basis-in-semisimple-case/#Chapter3___Theorem3___6___2":{"contents":"Theorem 3.6.2. (i) Characters of (distinct) irreducible finite dimensional representations of A are linearly independent.\n\n(ii) If A is a finite dimensional semisimple algebra, then these characters form a basis of (A/[A, A])^*.\n\nProof. (i) If V_1, \\ldots, V_r are nonisomorphic irreducible finite dimensional representations of A, then the map\n\n\\rho_{V_1} \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus \\rho_{V_r} : A \\to \\operatorname{End} V_1 \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus \\operatorname{End} V_r\n\nis surjective by the density theorem, so \\chi_{V_1}, \\ldots, \\chi_{V_r} are linearly independent. (Indeed, if \\sum \\lambda_i \\chi_{V_i}(a) = 0 for all a \\in A, then \\sum \\lambda_i \\operatorname{Tr}(M_i) = 0 for all M_i \\in \\operatorname{End}_k V_i. But each \\operatorname{Tr}(M_i) can range independently over k, so it must be that \\lambda_1 = \\cdots = \\lambda_r = 0.)\n\n(ii) First we prove that [\\operatorname{Mat}_d(k), \\operatorname{Mat}_d(k)] = \\mathfrak{sl}_d(k), the set of all matrices with trace 0. It is clear that [\\operatorname{Mat}_d(k), \\operatorname{Mat}_d(k)] \\subseteq \\mathfrak{sl}_d(k). If we denote by E_{ij} the matrix with 1 in the ith row of the jth column and 0's everywhere else, we have [E_{ij}, E_{jm}] = E_{im} for i \\neq m and [E_{i,i+1}, E_{i+1,i}] = E_{ii} - E_{i+1,i+1}. Now \\{E_{im}\\} \\cup \\{E_{ii} - E_{i+1,i+1}\\} forms a basis in \\mathfrak{sl}_d(k), so indeed [\\operatorname{Mat}_d(k), \\operatorname{Mat}_d(k)] = \\mathfrak{sl}_d(k), as claimed.\n\nBy semisimplicity, we can write A = \\operatorname{Mat}_{d_1}(k) \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus \\operatorname{Mat}_{d_r}(k). Then [A, A] = \\mathfrak{sl}_{d_1}(k) \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus \\mathfrak{sl}_{d_r}(k), and A/[A, A] \\cong k^r. By Theorem\n3.3.1, there are exactly r irreducible representations of A (isomorphic to k^{d_1}, \\ldots, k^{d_r}, respectively) and therefore r linearly independent characters on the r-dimensional vector space A/[A, A]. Thus, the characters form a basis. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.6. Characters of representations\u0009Linear independence of characters; basis in semisimple case","header":"Linear independence of characters; basis in semisimple case","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___6___-Characters-of-representations/Linear-independence-of-characters_SEMI_-basis-in-semisimple-case/#Chapter3___Theorem3___6___2"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/Characters-and-tensor-products-for-Heisenberg-group/#Chapter4___Problem4___12___9":{"contents":"Problem 4.12.9. Find the characters and tensor products of irreducible complex representations of the Heisenberg group from Problem 4.12.2.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.12. Problems\u0009Characters and tensor products for Heisenberg group","header":"Characters and tensor products for Heisenberg group","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/Characters-and-tensor-products-for-Heisenberg-group/#Chapter4___Problem4___12___9"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Section-5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Section-5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Section-5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"A finite group whose order is a product of powers of two primes is solvable.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_after_Theorem2.1.2/Derived5; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.4.3; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Introduction_5.4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.4.3; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.4. Burnside's theorem\u0009Section 5.4: Burnside's theorem\u0009Section 5.4: Burnside's theorem\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Section-5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Section-5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Section-5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Section-6___1-heading___-Problems/#Chapter6___Section6___1_heading___heading-1":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.1. Problems\u0009Section 6.1 heading: Problems\u0009Section 6.1 heading: Problems","header":"6.1. Problems","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Section-6___1-heading___-Problems/#Chapter6___Section6___1_heading___heading-1"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Example-6___4___9___-Roots-for-A____N-1___-and-other-Dynkin-types/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___4___-Roots--Example-6___4___9___-Roots-for-A____N-1___-and-other-Dynkin-types--Example-6___4___9___-Roots-for-A____N-1___-and-other-Dynkin-types--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Supplying eight and the adj field produces a finite set whose cardinality is one hundred twenty.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Example6.4.9; role=supporting]\n\nThe set obtained with parameter seven from the adj field is finite and has sixty-three elements.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Example6.4.9; role=supporting]\n\nUsing six as the parameter and the adj field as input gives a finite set with thirty-six elements.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Example6.4.9; role=supporting]\n\nFor every natural number at least four, the specified set is finite and has exactly n * (n - 1) elements.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Example6.4.9; role=supporting]\n\nThe auxiliary set has cardinality n * (n + 1) / 2.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Example6.4.9; role=supporting]\n\nA function belongs to the singleton-difference set exactly when it is the difference of two unit singleton functions at distinct indices.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Example6.4.9; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.4. Roots\u0009Example 6.4.9: Roots for A_{N-1} and other Dynkin types\u0009Example 6.4.9: Roots for A_{N-1} and other Dynkin types\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Example-6___4___9___-Roots-for-A____N-1___-and-other-Dynkin-types/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___4___-Roots--Example-6___4___9___-Roots-for-A____N-1___-and-other-Dynkin-types--Example-6___4___9___-Roots-for-A____N-1___-and-other-Dynkin-types--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Tensor-product-of-complexes-and-K___nneth-formula/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology--Tensor-product-of-complexes-and-K___nneth-formula--Tensor-product-of-complexes-and-K___nneth-formula--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"There exist data and an isomorphism such that, in every degree, the map on homology induced by the second biproduct inclusion followed by the inverse isomorphism is an isomorphism.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Problem7.8.7; role=supporting]\n\nThe composite of two differentials in the tensor product complex is zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Problem7.8.7; role=supporting]\n\nIdentifies the homology of the displayed tensor complex with the corresponding graded sigma object.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Problem7.8.7; role=supporting]\n\nGives an isomorphism between the two displayed functors of pairs of cochain complexes.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Introduction_7.8; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Problem7.8.7; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.8. Complexes and cohomology\u0009Tensor product of complexes and Künneth formula\u0009Tensor product of complexes and Künneth formula\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Tensor-product-of-complexes-and-K___nneth-formula/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology--Tensor-product-of-complexes-and-K___nneth-formula--Tensor-product-of-complexes-and-K___nneth-formula--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Section-9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___3":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.3. The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra\u0009Section 9.3: The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra","header":"Section 9.3: The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Section-9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___3"},"/Frontmatter/Blank-Page-2-___-Blank-page/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--Frontmatter--Blank-Page-2-___-Blank-page":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u0009Frontmatter","header":"Blank Page 2 — Blank page","id":"/Frontmatter/Blank-Page-2-___-Blank-page/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--Frontmatter--Blank-Page-2-___-Blank-page"}});